121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (There is a lot of confusion and misinformation when it comes to musical modes. Modes are basically as old as music, and have been used in many different ways across different cultures in the globe. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Differently than traditional scales \(which are simply a sequence of pitches\), modes are group of notes with a specific "taste", a "vibe", a hierarchy between the notes, and very specific melodic and harmonic behaviors. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Instead of simply looking as modes as "rearrangments" of the notes of a major scale \(which is commonly the way we learn modes in the Western world\), we would like to propose that we utilize more of Eastern approach, which sees in each mode a character, an entire emotional universe. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Understanding the characteristics and "ethos" \(emotions\) of each mode allows us to develop better ear training, arrangement ideas, fluency when improvising, and an overall musical intelligence. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (For the purpose of improvisation and composition, we will focus on 4 modes today. There are many more out there, so feel free to expand your practice into other directions and modes from other parts of the world. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (One of the main characteristics of modal music is that it is harmonically static; The chord movements are fairly reduced, and many times not even existent. This is very different from current Jazz chord-scale theory that assigns different modes to different chords, basically treating them as mere scales. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
