/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (For each mode, we will be highlighting an easy way to memorize its internal structure, identify the most important notes \(the notes that carry the emotional "ethos"\), and demonstrating a few harmonic progressions that can be used on each mode to highlight its unique sound. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (The major an minor scales can also be treated as modes \(respectively the ionian and aeolian\), but that implies using a rather static harmony without tonal relationships, which is quite rare.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (We have coined the term "PENDULUM CHORDS" to describe these simple chord progressions which can be repeated and looped endlessly helping to create the color universe of that mode. These chords are sometimes called modal color chords. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (Memorizing the pendulum chords of each mode is an extremely effective way to intuitively remember the modes and recognize modal passages in the repertoire around you. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (As always, the best way to internalize any musical concepts is by composing a piece of music utilizing that concept. When I took composition classes with the late Malcolm Peyton at the New England Conservatory, he  would make me  write dozens  of melodies )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (\(composition fragments\) in each different mode, trying to highlight the most important notes in each of those. Many of those fragments ended up becoming parts of my larger scale compositions.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
