157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (The Phrygian mode is rarely used as it is. But one specific variation of it has become very common in several different genres: the Spanish Phrygian, aka Flamenco scale. It is also very similar to the Hijaz makam in Middle Eastern music. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (One good way to remember the Spanish Phrygian mode is to think of a NATURAL MINOR SCALE WITH A LOWERED 2nd and an added MAJOR 3rd. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4 7 5 8 6]
>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (Having both a major and minor 3rd allow for a very unique and interesting sound; Chick Corea uses it a lot in his tune La Fiesta. The Spanish Phrygian carries an ethos of exoticism \(I hate this word, because it implies a certain orientalism to the mode\), but also desire, tension, drama, fate. Ravel also uses is a lot \(probably due to his basque origins\). )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9 11 10]
>>
endobj
160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (Here are some practice ideas:)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 308 0 R
/E (First explore each mode separetly, playing \(or recording\) the pendulum chords as a slow rubato progression and then improvising slow melodies over the chords, really feeling the different colors of each note and the gravity \(pull\) that each notes has. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
162 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 309 0 R
/E (Practice quickly transposing modes to different keys. For example, try to quickly play a Ab lydian with no mistakes. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
163 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 310 0 R
/E (Connecting modes: choose a pair o random modes in different keys, e.g. D spanish phrygian and G dorian. Stay on each of them for about 20 seconds and then shift to the other. Try the same thing with a harder pair of modes, for example Eb mixolydian and A lydian; You will have to find common tones and seamless ways to connect those two modes. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 311 0 R
/E (Practice playing a tune such as Flamenco Sketches by Miles Davis, which contains 5 different modes over which you can improvise. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 312 0 R
/E (Try establishing an ostinato using the notes of the mode. Record that ostinato, or play it on your left hand, while improvising over it. Take as an example the Children Songs of Chick Corea, especially No. 3 or No. 6 as a reference. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 106 0 R
/Pg 16 0 R
/A [313 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 314 0 R
/E (Explore the same exercises over modes that are less common, such as the whole-tone scale, the octatonic, double augmented scales, neapolitan scales, different maqam from the middle east, ragas from India, Half-Tizita scale from Ethiopia. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 315 0 R
/E (Experiment creating unique voicings within a mode. Pick an unusual group of notes from the mode \(avoiding conventional 3rds\), and move this voicing up and down the mode, creating unique colors. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 316 0 R
/E (Apply odd meters to modal ostinati, trying to emulate the sound of Balkan modal music \(very common in the music of Tigran Hamasyan or Avishai Cohen\). )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 317 0 R
/E (Create "Modal Parodies", in which you take a well known piece \(any genre\) and play it only using the notes of a given mode. I recommend starting with an easy mode. For example, take a simple tune like Donna Lee, or Fur Elise, and play it all in D dorian \(white notes on the piano\). The harmony will feel static, but that's the point. Finding new textures, figurations and ideas within the            constraint of the mode)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
